      "id": "waa4t8b",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "INTRODUCTION TO ELECTRICITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge (I = Q/t), with its SI unit being the Ampere.",
        "Electric potential difference (V) is the work done per unit charge in moving it between two points in a circuit.",
        "Ohm's Law establishes that the current passing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it, provided temperature is constant.",
        "Resistance depends on the length, cross-sectional area, material resistivity, and temperature of the conductor.",
        "Joule's Heating Effect states that heat generated is directly proportional to the square of current, resistance, and time (H = I²Rt)."
      ],
      "id": "3cnhk3v"
    },
